  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)$ and $g(t)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9=\\pi $\n- $\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9=0$\n- $f(t)\u22a5g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={[-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)}{2}|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u20610}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
